WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.484
I first came in 1993,

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.853
but I've been coming ten years in a row now,

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.555
since 2003.

00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:24.724
Well, since last century, really.

00:00:24.725 --> 00:00:26.259
It's been a long time.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.327
I started here as a graduate student working on squid

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:29.595
and doing electrophysiology.

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:31.330
And it's kind of evolved through the years,

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.666
to now we do... my wife and I come and do high-end imaging

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:35.234
and teac in the courses.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:37.537
So, it's been quite a long time.

00:00:37.538 --> 00:00:39.405
I have only been coming to the MBL probably since I was a postdoc,

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:42.441
so it's not as long... nearly as long as my husband Jim has been coming.

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.343
And I started coming more with him

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:45.978
because he was coming during the summers.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:48.080
So, we tried to find a way to come and bring our family.

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:51.484
So, I took the physiology course back in 2005.

00:00:51.485 --> 00:00:54.887
And I've been back every year ever since. Yeah.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.522
So, this is my eighth year here.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:01:00.459
I think this is my fifth year.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.428
The first time I came to the MBL was 1989,

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.365
to the New England Society for Electron Microscopy meeting.

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.734
I was a master's student,

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.237
and I saw Keith Porter give a talk

00:01:11.238 --> 00:01:13.506
on the stage of Lillie Auditorium.

00:01:13.507 --> 00:01:15.374
So, I don't know how many summers...

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:17.510
and I think I've been here at least for a couple days

00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:19.312
every single summer since then.

00:01:19.313 --> 00:01:21.047
I think this is around my seventh year.

00:01:21.048 --> 00:01:24.350
And it's... it's really an incredible honor to be here.

00:01:24.351 --> 00:01:26.652
It's something where I know lots of people

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.088
that are knockin' on the door and hopin' to get here,

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:30.990
and I count myself incredibly lucky.

00:01:30.991 --> 00:01:35.528
Well, I fell in love with the MBL as a physiology student in 1998.

00:01:35.529 --> 00:01:37.763
So, I spent six weeks here,

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.800
and had it in my head that I really, someday, imagined coming back.

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:44.370
And then, serendipitously, through really a chance conversation

00:01:44.371 --> 00:01:48.241
with Tim Mitchison at an ASCB meeting,

00:01:48.242 --> 00:01:52.178
the opportunity arose to start collaborating and coming...

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:54.947
collaborating with scientists at the MBL who are resident scientists.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:59.051
And then, basically, I had the opportunity to start coming

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.486
in the summer.

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.055
And now, I'm... I'm having a hard time imagining life

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:05.625
before I came to MBL in the summer.

00:02:05.626 --> 00:02:08.761
I took the course ten years ago, now,

00:02:08.762 --> 00:02:11.297
and still maintain collaborations

00:02:11.298 --> 00:02:13.165
with the people that I met here

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.134
because, in this environment with so much science,

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.736
so much excellent science going on,

00:02:16.737 --> 00:02:19.505
and the development of new techniques,

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.907
cutting-edge techniques,

00:02:20.908 --> 00:02:23.809
you really get ideas, you know, with the people that...

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.778
that are here,

00:02:25.779 --> 00:02:27.580
who are sharing this special summer time,

00:02:27.581 --> 00:02:29.515
and... and those relationships persist,

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:31.584
and that really has a powerful impact

00:02:31.585 --> 00:02:33.586
on my research and the research of many others.

00:02:33.587 --> 00:02:36.556
The level of interaction is way higher

00:02:36.557 --> 00:02:38.157
than it is in the research labs.

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:41.327
So, it's nonstop,

00:02:41.328 --> 00:02:46.399
fully saturating human, scientific interaction.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.502
And it's like the probability of...

00:02:49.503 --> 00:02:51.370
the higher the interaction level,

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.906
the higher the probability that

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.208
you'll get more than one inspiration.

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:59.912
I like coming to teach because I remember for this short period each year

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.914
what fun it is to do research.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.884
I get to block everything out and just think about science.

00:03:04.885 --> 00:03:07.420
I think that the courses are a great opportunity

00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:09.222
to basically interact with students.

00:03:09.223 --> 00:03:10.690
You basically get input from students.

00:03:10.691 --> 00:03:13.292
And a lot of times, you know, their ideas are...

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.361
they spark new ideas with you.

00:03:15.362 --> 00:03:18.464
And it's a great opportunity to just get more feedback.

00:03:18.465 --> 00:03:21.133
I know how cool it is to take the course

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:23.002
from first-hand experience,

00:03:23.003 --> 00:03:25.371
and it really, really...

00:03:25.372 --> 00:03:28.508
I do strongly believe that it changed the way I do science.

00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:30.710
I came here to learn more about biology

00:03:30.711 --> 00:03:32.912
and ended up falling in love with cell biology,

00:03:32.913 --> 00:03:34.981
and in particular the cytoskeleton.

00:03:34.982 --> 00:03:38.351
So, I came to learn a little bit, and I left

00:03:38.352 --> 00:03:40.853
having completely changed my life.

00:03:40.854 --> 00:03:42.889
If my life was a mathematical function,

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.224
the two summ...

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:48.160
two months that I spent in the physiology course in 1993

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:52.698
was a... was a discontinuity, or a singularity,

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:56.068
that left the shape of my life being completely different after the course.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.438
I've been coming down to the MBL for three summers

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:01.474
as a co-director of a course,

00:04:01.475 --> 00:04:02.742
and that's been an amazing experience.

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:04.510
So, the thing I really love about these courses

00:04:04.511 --> 00:04:08.047
is that we have a mixture of students, postdocs, and faculty

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:11.150
who come from around the world

00:04:11.151 --> 00:04:12.852
and they spend three weeks here,

00:04:12.853 --> 00:04:14.787
where they interact with each other

00:04:14.788 --> 00:04:18.024
and learn a lot from the faculty who pass through,

00:04:18.025 --> 00:04:19.859
from a course,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.761
but learn a ton from each other.

00:04:21.762 --> 00:04:23.429
And we learn a ton from them too.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.932
It's not just about, you know, the everyday interactions,

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:27.366
you know, with the speakers.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.335
It's not only about, you know,

00:04:29.336 --> 00:04:31.370
talking to other faculty, talking to people about their research.

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:33.706
It's also the fact, you know,

00:04:33.707 --> 00:04:35.508
you get to teach in an environment

00:04:35.509 --> 00:04:37.877
where people really think hard about what they're doing,

00:04:37.878 --> 00:04:41.047
and they force you to think even harder.

00:04:41.048 --> 00:04:43.182
So, I... I guess it's also a personal belief

00:04:43.183 --> 00:04:46.385
that through teaching one also gets to think harder

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:49.088
about the problems that one is thinking about

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:52.725
and try to make connections that you maybe didn't make originally.

00:04:52.726 --> 00:04:55.394
I always learn something new every year.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.697
I always learn things I never imagined that I would learn.

00:04:57.698 --> 00:04:59.866
I especially love teaching

00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:01.267
because I learn a lot from the students.

00:05:01.268 --> 00:05:02.869
I learn more from the students than from anyone else,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.306
just from hearing the research that they're doing back home

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:08.975
and interacting with them on a day-to-day basis,

00:05:08.976 --> 00:05:11.244
when we're trying to do experiments together...

00:05:11.245 --> 00:05:13.446
they always surprise me with their ingenuity,

00:05:13.447 --> 00:05:17.650
and I come away with a... completely energized, in a...

00:05:17.651 --> 00:05:19.652
with a totally new perspective on my work.

00:05:19.653 --> 00:05:23.489
I mean, I like coming here because I don't think I've ever come here

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.291
and not gotten a new idea.

00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:27.960
I... you know, there's so much going on here.

00:05:27.961 --> 00:05:29.962
I bounce off ten million people,

00:05:29.963 --> 00:05:32.598
and I bounce a million ideas off of people.

00:05:32.599 --> 00:05:39.338
I don't get anything done in a concrete sense,

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:42.041
but what I do get is inspiration and new ideas.

00:05:42.042 --> 00:05:44.911
And that's... I mean, that's worth more than,

00:05:44.912 --> 00:05:49.682
you know, a hundred uninspired experiments

00:05:49.683 --> 00:05:51.083
for the rest of the year.

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:54.287
The reasons I come here are partly to do my own experiments

00:05:54.288 --> 00:05:56.455
with my own hands,

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:59.258
which... once you become a PI of a lab group,

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:01.694
it becomes harder and harder to get out of the office

00:06:01.695 --> 00:06:03.095
and at the bench.

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:04.830
And here, I sit behind a microscope

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.465
and I look at real cells growing.

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:08.534
I do experiments,

00:06:08.535 --> 00:06:11.170
which gives me just the thrill for...

00:06:11.171 --> 00:06:13.206
for why I fell in love with science.

00:06:13.207 --> 00:06:17.109
And I get to rediscover that all over again, myself.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.579
And just to come down and almost be a postdoc again.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.714
To, like, sit in on seminars

00:06:21.715 --> 00:06:23.416
and hear what other people are doing.

00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:27.787
And... and I started a new collaboration before lunch my first day here.

00:06:27.788 --> 00:06:30.523
So, I mean, just having those sort of informal interactions

00:06:30.524 --> 00:06:31.991
with a new group of scientists

00:06:31.992 --> 00:06:33.726
is really energizing and really recharging.

00:06:33.727 --> 00:06:36.262
And... and it's been awesome.

00:06:36.263 --> 00:06:40.666
I'm hoping I can do this, you know, every summer, if possible.

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:42.235
It's been really fantastic.

00:06:42.236 --> 00:06:44.437
You have a critical mass of people

00:06:44.438 --> 00:06:45.638
that you would never have access to anywhere else.

00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:47.640
And also, everyone is very, very open.

00:06:47.641 --> 00:06:51.010
Here... it's a very open, sharing community,

00:06:51.011 --> 00:06:52.812
in terms of the ideas and the people.

00:06:52.813 --> 00:06:54.680
You can go in anybody's lab and ask them questions.

00:06:54.681 --> 00:06:56.883
You see seminars on things you wouldn't normally go to,

00:06:56.884 --> 00:06:58.251
because they're right here.

00:06:58.252 --> 00:06:59.952
And so, you get many more ideas.

00:06:59.953 --> 00:07:02.755
So, I would say about coming here that's so amazing,

00:07:02.756 --> 00:07:05.224
just... I can illustrate it with a story.

00:07:05.225 --> 00:07:07.193
I got off the bus three days ago.

00:07:07.194 --> 00:07:09.629
And between the time I got off the bus

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.097
and I got to my house,

00:07:11.098 --> 00:07:13.299
I ran into three people and already had two meetings planned,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.601
including a dinner meeting,

00:07:15.602 --> 00:07:17.937
and met somebody I hadn't met before and we talked in...

00:07:17.938 --> 00:07:19.672
really amazing science.

00:07:19.673 --> 00:07:22.842
So, there's just so many people here that are available.

00:07:22.843 --> 00:07:25.311
I really recharge my battery, scientifically.

00:07:25.312 --> 00:07:28.314
Chance encounters with someone on the sidewalk

00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:30.716
have completely changed, sometimes,

00:07:30.717 --> 00:07:33.152
the course of my experiments,

00:07:33.153 --> 00:07:34.554
and that sort of only happens at MBL,

00:07:34.555 --> 00:07:37.890
where we get this critical mass of great people wandering around,

00:07:37.891 --> 00:07:40.259
excited to talk about what problems

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:41.727
they're interested in.

00:07:41.728 --> 00:07:44.163
And so, it's an addiction,

00:07:44.164 --> 00:07:46.799
but I think it's a healthy addiction.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.104
The MBL is some sort of magical magnet

00:07:52.105 --> 00:07:55.274
that draws the best and...

00:07:55.275 --> 00:07:57.276
some of the best and most interesting scientists

00:07:57.277 --> 00:07:58.911
from around the world.

00:07:58.912 --> 00:08:03.783
And it's... so, in some sense, it's a crossroads,

00:08:03.784 --> 00:08:05.284
but it's really a destination.

00:08:05.285 --> 00:08:08.354
It's a place where lots of people come together from different places

00:08:08.355 --> 00:08:09.822
and, just for the summer months,

00:08:09.823 --> 00:08:12.592
assemble labs and interact as colleagues.

00:08:12.593 --> 00:08:15.161
I like coming here because

00:08:15.162 --> 00:08:18.831
it's a way to kind of just get away from the everyday things

00:08:18.832 --> 00:08:21.234
that are pulling on you

00:08:21.235 --> 00:08:22.768
and try new things.

00:08:22.769 --> 00:08:24.337
And there are people here to try new things with,

00:08:24.338 --> 00:08:25.972
and there's equipment here to try new things with.

00:08:25.973 --> 00:08:28.374
So, it's a way to explore wild ideas.

00:08:28.375 --> 00:08:30.510
Kind of spend a couple of weeks

00:08:30.511 --> 00:08:32.211
and see if you can get them to work.

00:08:32.212 --> 00:08:35.348
And you would never make the time to do that

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:38.484
in your everyday life, at your everyday institution.

00:08:38.485 --> 00:08:40.486
Another thing is that this is a place to play.

00:08:40.487 --> 00:08:44.056
And what I mean by that is that science really is about curiosity

00:08:44.057 --> 00:08:46.259
and investigating things because you find them interesting.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.161
And our lives at home are often colored

00:08:49.162 --> 00:08:50.830
by completely different considerations.

00:08:50.831 --> 00:08:53.699
So, every summer, we come here, and we try stuff.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.868
It's an invigorating, exciting place.

00:08:55.869 --> 00:08:58.137
It's got all kinds of great people, great equipment.

00:08:58.138 --> 00:09:00.139
I'm reminded of a quote from Sherlock Holmes,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:03.242
where he said that when you rule out the impossible,

00:09:03.243 --> 00:09:05.011
whatever remains, no matter how improbable,

00:09:05.012 --> 00:09:06.412
must be the truth.

00:09:06.413 --> 00:09:07.880
And so, you come to MBL to try the impossible.

00:09:07.881 --> 00:09:10.283
And it's a short time, sometimes, that you...

00:09:10.284 --> 00:09:11.484
the impossible works.

00:09:11.485 --> 00:09:14.921
And that's what's cool about this place, is that it... you can try it,

00:09:14.922 --> 00:09:16.822
and then you can go away and carry on with other things,

00:09:16.823 --> 00:09:18.457
or you get new ideas, and it's just...

00:09:18.458 --> 00:09:20.760
it's just the place to try it and have fun.

00:09:20.761 --> 00:09:24.830
But here, creative students, hard working,

00:09:24.831 --> 00:09:27.366
loads of equipment, and lots of reagents

00:09:27.367 --> 00:09:29.368
mean that you can try lots of things, iterate,

00:09:29.369 --> 00:09:32.104
and eventually find a solution to the problem.

00:09:32.105 --> 00:09:34.540
There's something really wonderful

00:09:34.541 --> 00:09:37.476
about an atmosphere where you try to do things

00:09:37.477 --> 00:09:39.412
just because you're curious about them,

00:09:39.413 --> 00:09:41.214
just because you've been wanting to try something out.

00:09:41.215 --> 00:09:43.516
You're not worried about whether or not it will "work"

00:09:43.517 --> 00:09:44.650
or won't work, necessarily.

00:09:44.651 --> 00:09:46.219
You just want to see what will happen.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.020
You can you can easily get out of your comfort zone

00:09:48.021 --> 00:09:50.723
and, you know, you can think of the craziest experiment,

00:09:50.724 --> 00:09:53.192
and if... more or less, if you can put it together,

00:09:53.193 --> 00:09:55.228
people will help you do it.

00:09:55.229 --> 00:10:00.233
So, I think that really gave me a lot of confidence

00:10:00.234 --> 00:10:04.036
and it inspired in me a little bit of the... of the...

00:10:04.037 --> 00:10:05.705
of a spirit like, look, let's just go for it.

00:10:05.706 --> 00:10:08.774
You know, it's like... I mean, when you have an idea... okay...

00:10:08.775 --> 00:10:10.276
so, how can I get it to work?

00:10:10.277 --> 00:10:11.944
Let's just give it a try.

00:10:11.945 --> 00:10:13.813
I don't know of anywhere more magical.

00:10:13.814 --> 00:10:15.481
And so, even though it's certainly true that people like me,

00:10:15.482 --> 00:10:16.983
that are a little bit older,

00:10:16.984 --> 00:10:18.684
should come here,

00:10:18.685 --> 00:10:21.621
you know, I would say that the bottom line is that

00:10:21.622 --> 00:10:23.856
this is a rite of passage, you know, for students.

00:10:23.857 --> 00:10:25.458
Here's a test.

00:10:25.459 --> 00:10:28.594
If you see anyone who has taken a course at the MBL,

00:10:28.595 --> 00:10:30.863
or done summer research here,

00:10:30.864 --> 00:10:32.498
taken the physiology course, say,

00:10:32.499 --> 00:10:34.567
just mention the word MBL to them, or the word Woods Hole,

00:10:34.568 --> 00:10:37.403
and see if they don't get kind of a misty, faraway look in their eyes

00:10:37.404 --> 00:10:39.772
and start talking about the magic of the place.

00:10:39.773 --> 00:10:42.241
They'll start to sound a little bit like maybe...

00:10:42.242 --> 00:10:45.578
some sort of religious fanatic talking about a cult, maybe.

00:10:45.579 --> 00:10:48.114
If you haven't been here, that might be what they sound like.

00:10:48.115 --> 00:10:50.082
But if you have been here,

00:10:50.083 --> 00:10:53.085
it will sound more like an expatriate

00:10:53.086 --> 00:10:54.954
talking about their homeland,

00:10:54.955 --> 00:10:59.492
you know, a place where they feel some deep connection to the...

00:10:59.493 --> 00:11:10.337
to what they're doing.

